ollowing relationships for the roots of the polynomial \\(x^3 - 10x^2 + 55x - c = 0\\):\n1. The sum of the roots \\(a + b + c = 10\\).\n2. The sum of the product of the roots taken two at a time \\(ab + bc + ca = 55\\).\n3. The product of the roots \\(abc = c\\).\n\nWe are given that the product of two of the roots is 30. Without loss of generality, let's assume \\(ab = 30\\). We need to find the value of \\(c\\).\n\nFrom the product of the roots, we have:\n\\[abc = c.\\]\nSince \\(ab = 30\\), we can substitute to get:\n\\[30c = c.\\]\nThis simplifies to:\n\\[30c - c = 0 \\implies 29c = 0 \\implies c = 0.\\]\n\n\n</partial_draft>","role":"user"},{"content":"You may re-analyze the problem and rewrite the solution from the beginning.","role":"user"}],"rendered_sha256":"ba28dcec64868ef0e17d6a173cdd5bbbbc49e2dcd7efe57e12eab3682fa6ce79"},"seconds":36.54704737663269,"seed":275732649736073787,"stop":"eos","text":"To solve for \\(c\\) in the given polynomial equation \\(x^3 - 10x^2 + 55x - c = 0\\) given that two of the roots have a product of 30, we will use Vieta's formulas and properties of polynomial roots. Let's denote the roots of the polynomial by \\(a\\), \\(b\\), and \\(d\\).\n\nFrom Vieta's formulas, we know the following relationships for the roots of the polynomial \\(x^3 - 10x^2 + 55x - c = 0\\):\n1. The sum of the roots \\(a + b + d = 10\\).\n2. The sum of the product of the roots taken two at a time \\(ab + ad + bd = 55\\).\n3. The product of the roots \\(abd = c\\).\n\nWe are given that the product of two of the roots is 30. Without loss of generality, let's assume \\(ab = 30\\). We need to find the value of \\(c\\).\n\nFrom the product of the roots, we have:\n\\[abd = c.\\]\nSince \\(ab = 30\\), we can substitute to get:\n\\[30d = c.\\]\n\nNext, we use the equation for the sum of the product of the roots taken two at a time:\n\\[ab + ad + bd = 55.\\]\nSubstituting \\(ab = 30\\), we get:\n\\[30 + ad + bd = 55.\\]\nThis simplifies to:\n\\[ad + bd = 25.\\]\nFactoring out \\(d\\), we have:\n\\[d(a + b) = 25.\\]\n\nFrom the sum of the roots, we have:\n\\[a + b + d = 10.\\]\nSolving for \\(a + b\\), we get:\n\\[a + b = 10 - d.\\]\nSubstituting this into the equation \\(d(a + b) = 25\\), we have:\n\\[d(10 - d) = 25.\\]\nThis simplifies to:\n\\[10d - d^2 = 25.\\]\nRearranging terms, we get a quadratic equation:\n\\[d^2 - 10d + 25 = 0.\\]\nThis can be factored as:\n\\[(d - 5)^2 = 0.\\]\nSo, \\(d = 5\\).\n\nNow we can find \\(c\\) using \\(30d = c\\):\n\\[c = 30 \\cdot 5 = 150.\\]\n\nThus, the value of \\(c\\) is \\(\\boxed{150}\\)."},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{150}","correct":true,"format":"boxed","parseable":true,"retains_student_answer":false},"graded_text":"To solve for \\(c\\) in the given polynomial equation \\(x^3 - 10x^2 + 55x - c = 0\\) given that two of the roots have a product of 30, we will use Vieta's formulas and properties of polynomial roots. Let's denote the roots of the polynomial by \\(a\\), \\(b\\), and \\(d\\).\n\nFrom Vieta's formulas, we know the following relationships for the roots of the polynomial \\(x^3 - 10x^2 + 55x - c = 0\\):\n1. The sum of the roots \\(a + b + d = 10\\).\n2. The sum of the product of the roots taken two at a time \\(ab + ad + bd = 55\\).\n3. The product of the roots \\(abd = c\\).\n\nWe are given that the product of two of the roots is 30. Without loss of generality, let's assume \\(ab = 30\\). We need to find the value of \\(c\\).\n\nFrom the product of the roots, we have:\n\\[abd = c.\\]\nSince \\(ab = 30\\), we can substitute to get:\n\\[30d = c.\\]\n\nNext, we use the equation for the sum of the product of the roots taken two at a time:\n\\[ab + ad + bd = 55.\\]\nSubstituting \\(ab = 30\\), we get:\n\\[30 + ad + bd = 55.\\]\nThis simplifies to:\n\\[ad + bd = 25.\\]\nFactoring out \\(d\\), we have:\n\\[d(a + b) = 25.\\]\n\nFrom the sum of the roots, we have:\n\\[a + b + d = 10.\\]\nSolving for \\(a + b\\), we get:\n\\[a + b = 10 - d.\\]\nSubstituting this into the equation \\(d(a + b) = 25\\), we have:\n\\[d(10 - d) = 25.\\]\nThis simplifies to:\n\\[10d - d^2 = 25.\\]\nRearranging terms, we get a quadratic equation:\n\\[d^2 - 10d + 25 = 0.\\]\nThis can be factored as:\n\\[(d - 5)^2 = 0.\\]\nSo, \\(d = 5\\).\n\nNow we can find \\(c\\) using \\(30d = c\\):\n\\[c = 30 \\cdot 5 = 150.\\]\n\nThus, the value of \\(c\\) is \\(\\boxed{150}\\).","id":"intermediate_algebra:568"}
